==Dungeoneering bosses==
{{main|Dungeoneering/Bosses}}[[File:Gluttonous behemoth.png|thumb|200px|The [[gluttonous behemoth]], a boss found during Dungeoneering.]]
At the end of each [[floor]] of [[Daemonheim]] is a powerful boss monster that must be defeated in order to complete the dungeon. The levels and strengths of these bosses vary according to a combination of factors, including the dungeon's size and complexity rating, but they will otherwise always have the same qualities. [[Kal'Ger the Warmonger]] is the strongest boss in Daemonheim.

==Enrage==
Enrage is a mechanic with certain bosses, first introduced with [[Araxxi]], that increases the damage dealt by the bosses from both auto-attacks and special attacks. Some bosses have new or changed special attacks at certain enrage thresholds, and most bosses life points scale to enrage too.

Araxxor and Araxxi increase their enrage throughout the fight via various mechanics. After killing and looting Araxxi's corpse, the next fight with the pair will add an additional 20% enrage to the fight, stacking with every kill afterwards, capping at 300% (although additional enrage can still be obtained during the fight). Enrage also affects the drop rate of the [[araxyte pheromone]] and [[araxyte pet]], although the latter is only affected by enrage gained during the fight. An araxyte pheromone can be broken to reset their enrage level back to 0%.

On [[27 June]] [[2016]], another boss with enrage, [[Telos, the Warden]], was released. Telos' enrage functions similarly to Araxxor and Araxxi's, but with additional functions. Although the auto-attack scaling is significantly weaker (as a 300% enrage Telos deals far less damage than Araxxi), the damage dealt from his special attacks scales significantly higher. Various mechanics are added and/or modified as his enrage increases (such as a final phase in encounters of 100% enrage and higher). The higher Telos' enrage is, the more likely a unique can be rolled, although streaks still play a higher role in the drop rate in most cases. Telos does not possess any mechanics that increase his enrage during the fight; this is only applied whenever the player continues their streak after each kill, with a 5-20% enrage stack accumulating to the current level of enrage. Telos' health caps at 600,000 at 200%+ enrage and 150,000 health at 300%+ enrage during the fifth phase.

The [[Arch-Glacor]] boss fight features enrage and streak mechanics largely similar to those of Telos. Unlike Telos, the Arch-Glacor has no health cap, resulting in it possessing 3,300,000 health at 4000% enrage, the highest of any solo boss in game and is widely considered the hardest challenge outside of soloing team bosses. The only mechanic that initially changes with enrage is the addition of a [[Bolstered Glacyte]] at 250% enrage and higher. The next change comes in at 2500% and higher enrage, in which the health of the [[Icy Arm]]s and glacytes begin to scale alongside the boss.

[[The Zamorakian Undercity]] utilises an enrage system, with the selected enrage affecting the entire dungeon. This increases all enemies' health and damage, up to a cap at 250%. All enrages from 0% to 4000% are unlocked by default, with additional enrages up to 60,000% unlocked in increments of 100% per successful kill. At specific enrage intervals, enemies are also replaced with stronger variants intended to make clearing the dungeon significantly harder. Like Telos, Zamorak has an additional phase at encounters of 100%+ enrage, and mechanics begin to be modified starting at 500% enrage up to 1600% enrage. Zamorak's health caps at 1,600,000 per player at 1740%+ enrage and 250,000 health at 1000%+ enrage during the seventh phase.

[[Amascut, the Devourer]] also has an enrage system. However, she has 2 additional phases at 100% enrage instead of 1. She may also be fought in preset enrages of 100%, 500%, 750%, 1000%, 2000% and 4000%, all of which modify and/or enhance existing mechanics at these thresholds. Unlike previous enrage bosses, players can only choose fixed enrages and it does not increase after defeating her. At 2000% and higher enrage, the [[Lone Soul]] debuff is applied onto all players, preventing them from benefitting off of [[Heal Other]], [[Heal Group]] and [[Intercept]].

[[The Ghrazi Blood Knights]] also use an enrage system that follows the very simialr rules as Amascut's enrage system. The prevention of certain group spells occurs at 1000% enrage instead.

Before [[20 April]] [[2026]], enrage at Telos, the Arch-Glacor and Zamorak functioned differently. Rather than having all enrages up to 4000% unlocked, enrages had to be reached first. While Zamorak still had a 60,000% enrage cap, successful kills allowed the player to climb in 10% enrage intervals rather than the current 100%. This meant hundreds of kills were required in order to unlock the maximum enrages.

==Bosses that prevent teleports==

During certain boss fights, the player is prevented from using teleports to escape. 

Prior to [[16 August]] [[2021]], most high-level bosses prevented players from teleporting, resulting in it being a fight to the death. These bosses included [[Nex: Angel of Death]], [[The Barrows: Rise of the Six]], [[Vorago]], [[Araxxor]] and [[Araxxi]], the [[Liberation of Mazcab]] raid, [[Telos, the Warden]] and hard mode [[Arch-Glacor]]. After the update, players can now safely teleport away from most bosses, apart from Telos and hard mode Arch-Glacor due to their unique reward mechanics.

